deep.reconstruction:
This page sits inside Euler's discrete model of a column. The column is two rigid arms, CA (top) and CB (bottom), each of length a and weight M. They are joined at C by an elastic hinge ('elastrum') of stiffness Ee. A horizontal force C acts at the joint, which is displaced horizontally from the vertical AB by CO = aθ, with θ small.

The reactions are:
- a horizontal force A at the top, which keeps A on the vertical;
- a horizontal force B at the foot;
- a vertical floor reaction BO = 2M at the foot.

The total weight 2M acts at the common centre of gravity G, with OG = ½aθ.

The previous page gave horizontal force balance: A + B = C.

This page first takes moments about A. The forces A and 2M pass through A (2M acts along the vertical AB). The remaining moments are:
- B, lever 2a: 2aB, sinistrorsum;
- C, lever a: Ca, dextrorsum;
- the weight 2M, lever ½aθ: Maθ, sinistrorsum.

Hence 2aB + Maθ = Ca. Together with A + B = C this gives A = ½C + ½Mθ and B = ½C − ½Mθ.

Euler remarks that if moments vanish about one axis they vanish about all. This is valid here because the force balance has already been imposed.

§11 then isolates the upper arm and takes moments about the joint C:
- the reaction A, lever a: Aa;
- the arm's own weight M, lever ½aθ: ½Maθ;
- both balanced by the hinge moment 2Eeθ, since the relative rotation of the two arms is 2θ.

So Aa + ½Maθ = 2Eeθ. Substituting A gives the governing equation ½aC + Maθ = 2Eeθ.

The next page checks this with the lower arm. It then gives θ = Ca/(4Ee − 2Ma) and the critical (buckling) condition 2Ee = Ma, at which the column deflects under its own weight with no lateral force.

The purpose is a two-degree-of-freedom rigid-link-and-spring model of self-weight buckling of a column. It serves as a heuristic for the continuous problem treated in the rest of E510.
